  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Thursday: An impressive looking frontal band \non satellite is pushing into western Oregon early this morning. \nThis approaching weather feature will tighten the northeast \npressure gradient with northeast winds picking up across the \nColumbia Basin this morning. The winds will act to break up the \nfog earlier than the previous mornings. Some areas may see a bit\nof sun, but clouds will increase in the afternoon as the \nfrontal band advances. The atmosphere will moisten up from the \ntop down Wednesday night, but the dynamics along the frontal \nband will be weakening as it moves across the forecast area. The\ntrack of the parent low southwest of the area with northeast \nwinds into the central portion of the east slopes of the \nCascades will result in favorable orographics, especially along \nthe ridge line that includes Blewett Pass and Mission Ridge. \nOrographics will be the dominant forcing mechanism for \nprecipitation even with the dynamics along the front weakening \non the synoptic scale. This will result in localized heavy snow \naccumulations in the higher terrain above 3,000 feet Wednesday \nnight through Thursday morning. A winter weather advisory is in \neffect for this period that includes Blewett Pass and Mission \nridge with accumulations of 4-8 inches expected, but local \naccumulations of 10 inches or more will be possible.\n\nThursday night through Saturday: A break between weather systems at \nthe end of the work week, although some lingering light \nprecipitation is expected over the Idaho Panhandle Thursday \nnight into Friday evening. Cooler temperatures closer to normal\nfor mid to late November with highs in the upper 30s to lower \n40s by Friday into Saturday. Low temperatures by this time are \nexpected to be in the mid to upper 20s for most locations. \nDepending on how much moisture we see with this next weather \nsystem Wednesday night into Thursday, we may see more in the way\nof fog in the valleys. That is still a bit uncertain though and\nunsure if we'll see as much fog as the past few days.\n\nSaturday night through Tuesday: Model guidance has been consistent \nwith another low pressure system to approach the region Saturday. \nThe track looks very similar to the system for mid week with the \ncenter of the low moving off of the coast of Oregon. This one looks \nto stall though off of the coast for the weekend and its proximity \nto the Northwest region will play a big part in how much moisture \ncomes with a weakening frontal band Saturday night into Sunday. \nTemperatures are expected to be a bit cooler and any moisture we get \nis expected to fall as snow for much of the lowlands. The question \nis will we get enough to produce more than just a dusting for much \nof the region, and that uncertainty still remains. I don't expect \nmuch impact from this weather system for the weekend, but the \npotential is there for an inch or two for places with minor travel \nimpacts. A decreasing trend in precipitation is expected around\nMonday, but models show additional energy pushing across off of\nthe eastern Pacific around Monday night into Tuesday that will \nbring the potential for additional wintry weather. /SVH",
